{
 "content": [
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "A log probability ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log \\p(E)"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " is simply the log function applied to a probability. Recall that a logarithm is the inverse of an exponent; ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log_b a = x"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " means that ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "b^x = a"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ". In this book, if a log doesn't have a written base, the default base is Euler's number ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "e"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ".",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "For example if ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\p(E) = 0.00001"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " then ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log \\p(E) = \\log(0.00001) \\approx -11.51"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ". Note that in this book, the default base is the natural base e. There are many reasons why log probabilities are an essential tool for digital probability: (a) computers can be rather limited when representing ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "marks": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "class": null,
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating-point_arithmetic",
        "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
        "target": "_blank"
       },
       "type": "link"
      }
     ],
     "text": "very small numbers",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "text": " and (b) logs have the wonderful ability to turn multiplication into addition, and computers are much faster at addition.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "logarithms",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Properties of Logarithms",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "content": [
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log(1) = 0"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log(e) = 1"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log(0) = -\\infty"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Log of the product: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log (xy) = \\log x + \\log y"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Log of an exponent: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log(x^c) = c \\cdot \\log x"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Log of a fraction: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "\\log(\\frac{1}{x^c}) = -c \\cdot \\log(x)"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "textAlign": "left"
     },
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Change of Base: ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": " \\log_b a = \\frac{\\log_x a}{\\log_x b}"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      },
      {
       "text": " for any ",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "attrs": {
        "showEditor": false,
        "value": "x"
       },
       "type": "math_inline"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
   "type": "borderedBox"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "representing-very-small-probabilities",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Products become Addition",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Since the log of the product is the sum of the logs, the product of probabilities ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\p(E)"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " and ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\p(F)"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " becomes addition in logarithmic space:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\log (\\p(E) \\cdot \\p(F) ) = \\log \\p(E) + \\log \\p(F)"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "This is especially convenient because computers are much more efficient when adding than when multiplying. It can also make derivations easier to write. This is especially helpful when you are dealing with many probabilities multiplied together:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\log \\prod_i \\p(E_i) = \\sum_i \\log \\p(E_i)"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": "logarithms",
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Representing Very Small Probabilities",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Computers have the power to process many events and consider the probability of very unlikely situations. While computers are capable of doing all the computation, the floating point representation means that computers cannot represent decimals to perfect precision. In fact, Python is unable to represent any probability smaller than 2.225e-308. On the other hand the log of that same number, -307.652, is very easy for a computer to store.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Why would you care? Often in the digital world, computers are asked to reason about the probability of data, or a whole dataset. For example, perhaps your data is words and you want to reason about the probability that a given author would write these specific words. While this probability is very small (we are talking about an exact document) it might be larger than the probability that a different author would write a specific document with specific words. For these sorts of small probabilities, if you use computers, you would need to use log probabilities.",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "id": null,
    "level": "3",
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Logs of Probabilities are Negative",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "heading"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "In our earlier example ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log(0.00001) \\approx -11.51"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " produced a negative number. That is not a coincidence. The log of any probability will be a negative number. The higher the probability, the closer that negative number will be to zero:",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "rawTex": "\\begin{aligned}\n0 &\\leq  \\p(E) \\leq 1 && \\text{Axiom 1 of probability} \\\\\n-\\infty &\\leq \\log \\p(E) \\leq 0 && \\text{Rule for log probabilities}\n\\end{aligned}"
   },
   "type": "block-tex"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Here is a graph of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "x"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " and ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log(x)"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " in the range of values that a probability can take on: ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "0 < x < 1"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ":",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "sourceBytes": 1277,
    "sourceSha256": "a54f234a3f5138d49666c9b5c09338cfcaea496046da5e0a01f77d02691053a2"
   },
   "type": "interactive-demo"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Notice that all of the log of all values are negative. What is hard to show on this chart is that as ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "x"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " gets closer and closer to 0, then ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log(x)"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " gets closer to ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "-\\infty"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ".",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "content": [
    {
     "text": "Recall that ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "\\log a = x"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ", with the implied natural base e, is the same as the statement ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "e ^ x = a"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ". In other words ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "x"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " is the exponent of ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "e"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " that produces ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "a"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": ". If ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "a"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " is a number between 0 and 1, what power should you raise ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "e"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " to in order to produce b? If you raise ",
     "type": "text"
    },
    {
     "attrs": {
      "showEditor": false,
      "value": "e^0"
     },
     "type": "math_inline"
    },
    {
     "text": " it produces 1. To produce a number less than 1, you must raise e to a power less than 0. ",
     "type": "text"
    }
   ],
   "type": "paragraph"
  },
  {
   "attrs": {
    "textAlign": "left"
   },
   "type": "paragraph"
  }
 ],
 "type": "doc"
}
